## 1. What is Myrmec

Myrmec is not just a tool for developers. The whole idea is to eliminate
developer dependency from day-to-day AI-assisted work. Once Myrmec is set up,
anybody can use the Control Plane UI to define and execute agentic workflows
without writing a single line of code.

Myrmec sits between your people and your AI models. It gives you structured,
repeatable, observable execution rather than ad-hoc prompting. The platform
is built around five core concepts:

- **Models and Providers** — Admin manages the list of subscribed models and
  the providers they come from (OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure, Bedrock,
  self-hosted). Token limits, cost caps, and access permissions are set at
  this level.
- **Agent Profiles** — Admin defines reusable profiles that combine a model,
  supported tools, capabilities, a system prompt, and a sandbox image.
  Profiles are the reusable building blocks that workflows and sessions are
  composed from.
- **Agents** — Admin registers and manages the actual agent instances, their
  credentials, and their health. Agents run inside pre-built sandbox
  containers, pull tasks over real-time channels, and execute them
  independently.
- **Knowledge Sources** — Admin defines organisational knowledge such as
  coding standards, architecture guidelines, policies, runbooks, and
  product documentation, indexed from git repositories, file uploads, web
  sources, and connectors (Confluence, Notion, SharePoint, Jira). This
  knowledge is retrieved on demand, with citations, so every execution
  benefits from the organisation's accumulated truth rather than the
  model's training data.
- **Workflows and Sessions** — Two ways to drive work: deterministic
  **workflows** (multi-step, scheduled or triggered) and **interactive
  sessions** (conversational, real-time, human-in-the-loop). Both run on
  the same governed runtime, share the same audit trail, and use the same
  knowledge and tools.

### The Myrmec wedge

Myrmec is the **governed runtime where agents read your org's truth and act
on it under audit**. Three things together make it different from anything
else on the market:

1. **Grounded in your org** — RAG over your git, docs, tickets, schemas, and
   policies. Every answer cites its sources.
2. **Acts safely in a sandbox** — Agents run in pre-built container images.
   The only things they can see are the tools you give them and the project
   workspace they are scoped to. They cannot exfiltrate, cannot reach
   arbitrary endpoints, cannot read other projects' secrets.
3. **Under audit, with approval gates** — Every retrieval, every tool call,
   every model call is logged. High-risk actions pause for explicit human
   sign-off via approval cards that show the exact diff, SQL, or command
   before it runs. AUDITOR role can replay any past session forensically.

### Platform roles

- **System Admin** — Sets up infrastructure, manages models, agent profiles,
  agents, users, and global knowledge sources.
- **Project Editor** — Creates and manages projects, configures
  project-level knowledge, builds workflows, and triggers executions.
- **Project Viewer** — Monitors live and historical executions, reviews
  logs, and audits outputs.

### Workflow execution

- A workflow is a sequence of steps, each assigned to an agent profile,
  with its own input, output, and acceptance criteria.
- When a Project Editor triggers a workflow execution the Control Plane UI
  shows a WYSIWYG live view of the flow, streaming logs and step-level
  status in real time.
- Every step result and log line is persisted so teams can audit behaviour
  and rerun failed steps without losing history.

### Interactive session execution

- A **session** is a conversational, real-time alternative to a scheduled
  workflow. The user chats with an agent profile that is bound to a project
  scope, a sandbox image, and a set of knowledge sources.
- Token-by-token streaming surfaces the agent's thinking, tool calls, and
  citations as it works — not a black-box "please wait".
- Every action the agent wants to take in the sandbox (run a command,
  execute SQL, apply a patch, hit an external API) can be gated through an
  **approval card** that shows the exact action; an APPROVER clicks Approve
  or Reject-with-comment.
- Cancel mid-stream, branch into a new turn, or hand off to another user
  — sessions are persistent, shareable within the project, and replayable
  by an AUDITOR.


## 2. Platform Features

The capabilities Myrmec ships with, grouped for easy reuse on a features
page or a comparison grid.

### Core building blocks

- **Models & Providers registry** — Centralised catalogue of subscribed
  models with per-model token caps, cost ceilings, and access rules.
- **Agent Profiles** — Reusable templates that bind a model, a set of
  tools, capabilities, a system prompt, and a sandbox image.
- **Agents** — Managed agent instances with registration, credentials,
  health monitoring, horizontal scale-out, and **container-based sandbox
  isolation** by default.
- **Knowledge Sources** — Project- and org-level knowledge indexed from
  git repositories, file uploads, web sources, and SaaS connectors
  (Confluence, Notion, SharePoint, Jira, database schemas). Retrieved on
  demand with **citations**, scoped by the same role model as the rest of
  the platform so a viewer on Project A cannot retrieve from Project B.

### Workflow engine

- **Visual workflow authoring** — Configure multi-step agentic workflows
  through the UI; no orchestration code required.
- **Live WYSIWYG execution view** — Streaming step status and logs as the
  workflow runs.
- **Persistent execution history** — Every step result, log line, and
  model call is stored for audit and re-run.
- **Human-in-the-Loop checkpoints** — Define approval steps where an agent
  pauses for explicit human sign-off before a high-risk action such as a
  production deployment.

### Interactive sessions

- **Conversational task execution** — A chat-style surface bound to a
  project, an agent profile, a sandbox image, and a knowledge scope.
- **Token-by-token streaming** — Watch the agent reason, retrieve, and act
  in real time; no opaque progress bar.
- **Inline approval cards** — Agents surface high-risk actions (shell
  commands, SQL queries, code patches, API calls) as approval requests
  with diff/preview, expiration, and reject-with-comment.
- **Cancel mid-stream** — Users can stop generation or tool execution at
  any time.
- **Session replay** — Every message, retrieval, tool call, and approval
  is persisted; AUDITOR role can replay any past session.
- **Shared sessions** — Sessions belong to projects, not individuals;
  teammates can join, review, and continue a conversation.

### Grounded answers (RAG)

- **Multi-source ingestion** — Git repositories (auto-synced via deploy
  keys), file uploads, web crawl, and connectors for Confluence, Notion,
  SharePoint, Jira, and database schemas.
- **Citations on every answer** — Responses include source pills that
  expand to the cited passage, file:line for code, or table:column for
  data dictionaries.
- **Scope-aligned retrieval** — Knowledge ACLs follow the platform's
  SYSTEM / GROUP / PROJECT scope model. A user can only retrieve chunks
  they are authorised to read.
- **Provider-agnostic embeddings** — Use Ollama, OpenAI, Cohere, or any
  other embedding model; swap without re-architecting.
- **Retrieval audit** — Every retrieval (query, chunk IDs, scores, used in
  which message) is logged for forensic replay.

### Governance & control

- **Sandbox isolation by default** — Agents execute inside pre-built
  container images. They can only see the tools you grant them and the
  project workspace they are scoped to. No laptop access, no arbitrary
  network egress, no cross-project secret leakage. Blast radius is bounded
  by design.
- **Infrastructure isolation** — Compute (GPUs, local nodes, cloud
  endpoints) and API connections are hidden from end users; workflow
  authors see capabilities, not credentials.
- **Auditability & provenance** — Every action an agent takes is logged,
  including which knowledge sources it referenced (with chunk IDs and
  similarity scores), every tool call, every model call, and every
  approval decision.
- **Multi-level token & cost quotas** — Define **and monitor** token
  budgets and spend caps at three layers that compose together: per
  **model** (global ceiling for an LLM), per **agent profile** (limit per
  capability/role), and per **project** (departmental or product-level
  budget). Live dashboards show consumption against each limit in real
  time, alerts fire as thresholds approach, and workflows are throttled
  or stopped before runaway autonomous loops blow through a budget.
- **Role-based access control** — Fine-grained roles at both system and
  project level keep permissions tight.
- **Shadow-AI mitigation** — Employees use the governed platform instead
  of unmanaged personal AI accounts; data only flows through
  Admin-approved models and secure knowledge sources.

### Flexibility & extensibility

- **Vendor-agnostic model swapping** — Swap the model behind an agent
  profile without changing any workflow; the business is protected from
  model lock-in.
- **Tool specialisation via SDK** — Build specialised agents that know how
  to talk to your internal APIs, legacy systems, or proprietary data
  sources.
- **Deterministic agentic flows** — Structured multi-step flows force the
  AI to follow a defined process, increasing reliability versus single
  prompts.
- **Horizontal scale** — Agents are stateless and can be deployed in any
  number of replicas across environments.


## 3. Use Cases

How organisations apply Myrmec in practice.

### Interactive sessions (governed, grounded, sandboxed)

These are the demoable scenarios that showcase the full Myrmec wedge —
conversational steering, grounded retrieval, sandboxed execution, and
approval-gated action.

- **Ops Runbook Copilot** — An on-call engineer chats: *"Reset the staging
  environment."* The agent retrieves the runbook from the `infra-docs`
  repo, proposes four steps, and surfaces each `kubectl` / `terraform`
  command as an approval card citing the runbook section. The engineer
  clicks Approve four times. The full session is replayable in the audit
  log. Nothing ran on the engineer's laptop.
- **NL→SQL Data Analyst** — An analyst asks: *"What's our MRR by region
  last quarter?"* The agent retrieves the data dictionary, the regional
  join definition, and the fiscal-quarter rule. Generates SQL → approval
  card with the query and estimated row count → approved → results stream
  back with citations to both the schema docs and the SQL that produced
  them. Database credentials never leave the sandbox.
- **Compliance Q&A** — *"What's our data-retention policy for EU customers?"*
  Agent retrieves from the policy repository, cites the GDPR clause and
  the matching internal policy section, and produces a structured answer.
  When the regulator asks how the answer was produced six months later,
  the AUDITOR replays the session.
- **Code-aware Operations** — *"Where do we handle webhook retry on Stripe
  failures?"* Agent searches the monorepo, returns the answer with
  file:line citations, and if asked, opens a PR fixing a bug it spots —
  with the diff surfaced as an approval card before the push.
- **Vendor Onboarding Assistant** — Procurement chats with an agent that
  is grounded in vendor policy and previous vendor records. Agent walks
  them through compliance questions, files Jira tickets via approved
  tools, and produces a final dossier for sign-off.

### Engineering & code

- **Code Generation** — Bring AI coding standards, architecture decisions,
