## 4. Why Myrmec

The value proposition for buyers and decision-makers.

- **The full "read → reason → act" loop, under audit.** Most platforms do
  one of the three. Glean reads. Copilot/Cursor act (on a laptop, without
  oversight). Bedrock Agents audit (but only through their own SDK).
  Myrmec is the only runtime that does all three together — grounded in
  your org's truth, executing in a sandbox, with every step replayable.
- **The answer to the security team's veto.** Regulated industries can't
  use Copilot or Cursor because of data egress and audit requirements.
  Myrmec runs agents in sandboxed containers, keeps credentials encrypted
  inside the platform, supports on-prem and air-gapped model providers,
  and logs every action. The CISO conversation is solved by design.
- **Centralised governance** of AI model configuration, access, and token
  limits. No more individual API keys scattered across developer machines.
- **Separation of infrastructure from business.** DevOps sets up and
  manages Myrmec; business users, analysts, and project teams use it to
  execute tasks without needing to understand the underlying
  infrastructure.
- **Organisation-level intelligence.** Knowledge sources let you encode
  your standards, architecture, and domain expertise once and apply them
  automatically across every workflow and every session, with citations on
  every answer so trust is verifiable, not assumed.
- **Institutional memory.** In most companies AI knowledge stays in a
  developer's private ChatGPT history. Myrmec captures prompt engineering
  and system logic into agent profiles that belong to the organisation.
- **Reduced individual dependency.** When AI-assisted work is encoded in a
  governed workflow rather than in a person's head or local prompt
  history, the organisation retains that capability permanently.
- **Full auditability.** Every workflow execution, step result, log line,
  and model call is recorded. Teams can reconstruct exactly what happened,
  when, and why.
- **Model flexibility.** Agent profiles are decoupled from specific
  models. Switching from one model or provider to another is a
  configuration change, not a code change.
- **Extendable by design.** The SDK allows teams to create agent images
  specialised for their own tools, data sources, and execution
  environments. Any capability that can be packaged as an agent can be
  plugged into Myrmec workflows.
- **Deterministic outcomes from non-deterministic models.** Structured
  agentic flows force the AI to follow a defined process, greatly
  increasing the reliability of the output.


## 4.5. Security & Compliance

Myrmec is built so the security and compliance teams can sign off without
turning AI adoption into an indefinite review cycle. The platform ships
the controls that regulated industries actually need, on by default,
visible to auditors.

### Identity, access, scope

- **Per-project access controls.** Every conversation, workflow, agent,
  knowledge base, secret, and quota is bound to a project. Users only see
  what their role grants on that project (`OWNER`, `EDITOR`, `VIEWER`,
  `APPROVER`, `AUDITOR`, `ORG_ADMIN`, `PLATFORM_ADMIN`).
- **Scope hierarchy.** SYSTEM → GROUP → PROJECT scopes apply consistently
  across roles, quotas, retrieval, and audit. Inheritance is explicit and
  walkable; nothing leaks across scopes by accident.
- **First-class agent identity.** Each agent instance authenticates with a
  scoped JWT; the engine treats agent traffic as a separate principal
  with its own RBAC role (`AGENT`). Stolen developer tokens can't
  impersonate agents, and stolen agent tokens can't impersonate humans.

### Human-in-the-loop on destructive actions

- **Risk-classified tools.** Every tool registered in Myrmec carries a
  `riskClass` (`SAFE`, `DESTRUCTIVE`, `IRREVERSIBLE`). Projects can flip
  on `autoHitlOnDestructive`: any agent attempting a destructive tool
  triggers an approval card surfaced to a designated approver.
- **Approval cards with full context.** Approvers see a diff (Monaco), the
  SQL, the shell command, or the structured JSON — whatever the agent
  proposes — before any side effect happens. Reject-with-comment loops
  back to the agent so it can revise.
- **Expiry by default.** Approval requests time out and auto-deny rather
  than silently leaving a side-effect window open.

### Quotas, rate limits, cost caps

- **Token + cost ceilings per scope.** Per-project, per-group, per-org
  quotas in either `TOKENS` or `COST_USD_CENTS`, evaluated against
  `DAILY`, `MONTHLY_CALENDAR`, or `LIFETIME` windows.
- **Child-tightens-only.** A project quota can never be more generous
  than its group's; a group quota can never exceed an org's. Validation
  fails the create/update, so a tight ceiling at the top is the hard
  ceiling everywhere.
- **80% warning band.** Users get a visible warning before the cap, not
  after, so they don't lose work to surprise blocks.
- **Telemetry mode.** `enforced=false` records consumption without
  blocking — useful for rolling out caps and confirming they're sized
  right before flipping enforcement on.
- **Hard stop with HTTP 429.** Once a cap is hit, both the chat dispatch
  and workflow start paths refuse the request with a `429 Too Many
  Requests` and a `Retry-After` header, so clients can back off cleanly.
- **Provider rate-limit retry.** When an upstream LLM returns 429, the
  agent SDK propagates the provider's `Retry-After` as a
  `MODEL_RATE_LIMITED` task failure with a retry hint, and the engine
  re-dispatches the task once the window closes (up to 5 retries, 30 s
  default backoff, 10 min cap). A single bursty rate limit no longer
  fails the whole workflow.

### Defence against prompt injection and data exfiltration

- **Outbound secret-leak scanner.** Every assistant turn is scanned for
  AWS keys, GitHub tokens, OpenAI keys, JWTs, and Slack tokens before
  the message leaves the engine. Modes: `BLOCK` (drop the turn), `REDACT`
  (replace the match with a mask), or `WARN` (audit only). Defaults to
  `REDACT` to keep agents useful while still containing leaks.
- **Untrusted-content wrapping.** Retrieval hits and tool outputs are
  wrapped in `<untrusted source="…" type="…">…</untrusted>` envelopes
  before being injected into prompts; embedded tags are neutralised so
  a poisoned document can't close the envelope and inject new
  instructions.
- **Tool description hash pinning.** Each tool's description is
  SHA-256 fingerprinted at admin-approval time. Any drift in the
  description clears the approval, so a compromised connector can't
  silently change a tool's persuasive text after the fact.

### Audit and observability

- **Append-only user-action audit log.** Logins (success and reason-coded
  failure), role grants, secret CRUD, quota CRUD, knowledge source add /
  remove — every privileged action lands in a separate `audit_log` table
  with actor, IP, user-agent, request id, and payload. No foreign keys
  on `actor_user_id` so deleting a user never wipes their history.
- **Execution snapshots from day one.** Every conversational turn that
  the engine dispatches writes a `CONVERSATION_TURN_DISPATCHED` snapshot
  (256 KB cap, per-project sampling) so the V2 replay timeline has months
  of real history to work against — not a green-field collection
  problem.
- **Centralised audit UI.** Platform admins filter the log by actor,
  resource, scope, and time without grepping Postgres.

### Deployment posture

- **On-prem or air-gapped.** The Community engine ships as a Spring Boot
  JAR with H2 for dev and Postgres for prod. The Enterprise build adds
  Ed25519-signed-license loading from a directory mount (no phone-home,
  no outbound entitlement checks). Both run in a customer's own VPC.
- **Multi-model.** Ship-with-yours: Ollama, vLLM, OpenAI, Anthropic.
  Mixing local and cloud is a runtime choice, not an architectural
  fork.
- **No vendor lock for governance.** Connectors, model providers,
  retrieval providers, secret stores, fanout buses, and license services
  are all SPI interfaces under `engine-spi`. The Community defaults are
  fully usable; Enterprise overrides are drop-in beans.


## 5. How Myrmec Compares

Myrmec sits in a category nobody else fully occupies: **a governed runtime
that grounds agents in your org's knowledge and lets them act in a
sandbox under audit**. Most products only cover one or two of those legs.

| Product category                        | Grounded in org knowledge | Acts (tools / code / data) | Sandboxed | Governed + audited | Multi-model / on-prem |
| --------------------------------------- | :-----------------------: | :------------------------: | :-------: | :----------------: | :-------------------: |
| Enterprise search assistants (Glean)    |            ✓✓             |             ✗              |    n/a    |      partial       |           ✗           |
| IDE copilots (Copilot, Cursor, Cody)    |          partial          |            ✓ (laptop)            |     ✗     |         ✗          |           ✗           |
| Autonomous coding agents (Devin)        |          partial          |             ✓              |     ✓     |      partial       |           ✗           |
| Cloud-vendor agent runtimes (Bedrock Agents, Vertex Agent Builder) |             ✗             |             ✓              |     ✓     |         ✓          |    ✗ (vendor lock)    |
| Low-code automation (Zapier, n8n)       |             ✗             |             ✓              |     ✗     |      partial       |        partial        |
| Orchestration frameworks (LangChain, AutoGen, CrewAI) |           depends           |          depends           |     ✗     |         ✗          |           ✓           |
| **Myrmec**                              |            **✓**            |            **✓**            |   **✓**   |        **✓**        |         **✓**         |

In plain English:

- **Unlike enterprise search assistants** (Glean) — Myrmec doesn't just
  answer questions; it executes work, under approval, with the same
  knowledge grounding.
- **Unlike IDE copilots** (GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Claude Code) — Myrmec is
  not tied to a developer's laptop. Agents run in governed sandboxes that
  the security team can actually approve.
- **Unlike autonomous coding agents** (Devin, Cognition) — Myrmec is
  multi-model, supports on-prem and air-gapped deployment, and is built
  for whole-org governance rather than a single coding loop.
- **Unlike cloud-vendor agent runtimes** (Bedrock Agents, Vertex Agent
  Builder) — Myrmec works across model providers and does not require
  buying into a single cloud's ecosystem.
- **Unlike low-code automation tools** (Zapier, n8n) — Myrmec is designed
  for intelligent multi-step agentic execution with model governance, not
  simple trigger-action pipelines.
- **Unlike raw orchestration frameworks** (LangChain, AutoGen, CrewAI) —
  Myrmec is a complete platform with UI, governance, RAG, audit, and
  sandboxing. Frameworks are construction kits; Myrmec is the building.


## 6. Architecture Overview

Myrmec has three main components:

- **Control Plane Engine** — A Spring Boot application backed by
  PostgreSQL. Manages all configuration, workflow definitions, executions,
  and audit records. Exposes a REST API consumed by the UI and agents.
- **Control Plane UI** — A React Native application that provides the
  management and execution interface. Runs in web and mobile contexts.
- **Agents** — Lightweight Python processes that register with the Control
  Plane, receive tasks over real-time channels, and execute them using
  their configured tools and models. Agents are stateless and
  horizontally scalable. Teams deploy their own agent images using the
  Myrmec SDK.


